Could be, could be not. I'd suggest, try and get a stack trace at the point 
the bus error occurs, then concentrate on the functions on that trace, 
starting at the end furthest from main().

BSD and gcc are not things I've used, so I hope others will correct me if I 
go wrong in this:

Presumably you got left a core file after the bus error. Try "gdb 
onethack14 core". If it swallows that, then all you need to do is get the 
stack trace. Try "backtrace". That should give you a list of function names 
(sorry if I'm teaching grandma to suck eggs here).

As the prototypes of sprintf() differ, it could be the code is expecting 
sprintf to return a pointer to the buffer the string is written to, while 
IIRC, the ISO sprintf() returns a byte count. Using a byte count as a 
pointer will usually result in either a bus error or a segmentation fault.

If the code is using the return value of sprintf as a pointer to a string, 
all you need to do is do the sprintf() first as a statement on its own, 
then replace the sprintf with its first argument. e.g.:

char* example(void) {
  ...
  return sprintf(buffer, format, value);
}

would become

char* example(void) {
  ...
  sprintf(buffer, format, value);
  return buffer;
}

This involves a lot of guesswork and could be wildly wrong, but debugging 
step-by-step by usenet would take a while ;-)

